1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,970
May 28, 1754. Dawn,

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:06,340
in a rocky glen in the forests of western

3
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:07,270
Pennsylvania.

4
00:00:08,051 --> 00:00:10,991
Forty Virginian soldiers and a dozen Native

5
00:00:10,991 --> 00:00:13,331
warriors creep into position around a French

6
00:00:13,331 --> 00:00:13,921
camp.

7
00:00:14,481 --> 00:00:16,551
Their commander is 22 years old.

8
00:00:16,801 --> 00:00:18,791
His name is George Washington.

9
00:00:19,647 --> 00:00:21,907
The fight that follows lasts about fifteen

10
00:00:21,907 --> 00:00:22,477
minutes.

11
00:00:23,098 --> 00:00:24,958
But it lights the fuse of a war that will

12
00:00:24,958 --> 00:00:26,568
spread across five continents,

13
00:00:27,218 --> 00:00:28,568
redraw the map of the world,

14
00:00:28,918 --> 00:00:30,208
and, twenty years later,

15
00:00:30,358 --> 00:00:32,228
help give birth to the United States.

16
00:00:32,939 --> 00:00:35,519
This is the story of the ambush at Jumonville

17
00:00:35,519 --> 00:00:35,909
Glen.

18
00:00:37,626 --> 00:00:41,006
In the 1750s, North America was split between

19
00:00:41,006 --> 00:00:42,176
two rival empires.

20
00:00:43,065 --> 00:00:45,145
Britain's thirteen colonies hugged the Atlantic

21
00:00:45,145 --> 00:00:48,085
coast: crowded, growing, and hungry for

22
00:00:48,085 --> 00:00:48,515
land.

23
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:51,090
France held a vast arc of territory,

24
00:00:51,090 --> 00:00:53,360
from Quebec, along the Great Lakes,

25
00:00:53,850 --> 00:00:54,780
down to New Orleans,

26
00:00:55,070 --> 00:00:57,720
linked by rivers and a thin chain of forts.

27
00:00:58,147 --> 00:01:00,987
Between them lay the Ohio Country: a rich

28
00:01:00,987 --> 00:01:02,977
wilderness of forests and rivers,

29
00:01:03,367 --> 00:01:04,617
home to the Shawnee,

30
00:01:04,667 --> 00:01:06,647
the Delaware, the Mingo and other nations,

31
00:01:06,647 --> 00:01:09,657
who traded with both sides and trusted neither.

32
00:01:10,264 --> 00:01:12,114
The powerful Iroquois Confederacy,

33
00:01:12,664 --> 00:01:15,164
the Six Nations, claimed authority over

34
00:01:15,164 --> 00:01:17,984
the region, and sent leaders like Tanacharison

35
00:01:17,984 --> 00:01:18,974
to speak for them.

36
00:01:19,442 --> 00:01:21,322
Whoever controlled its rivers controlled

37
00:01:21,322 --> 00:01:23,012
the heart of the continent.

38
00:01:23,696 --> 00:01:25,536
And the key to those rivers was a single

39
00:01:25,536 --> 00:01:27,996
point of land: the Forks of the Ohio,

40
00:01:27,996 --> 00:01:30,576
where the Allegheny and the Monongahela

41
00:01:30,576 --> 00:01:33,346
meet. Today, it is the city of Pittsburgh.

42
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:38,440
In 1753, the French began building forts

43
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:39,730
south from Lake Erie,

44
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:42,290
pushing into land that Virginia claimed.

45
00:01:42,891 --> 00:01:45,391
Virginia's governor, Robert Dinwiddie,

46
00:01:45,391 --> 00:01:47,151
needed someone to carry a warning to the

47
00:01:47,151 --> 00:01:50,161
French commander: leave, or be removed.

48
00:01:50,835 --> 00:01:53,495
He chose a 21-year-old surveyor and militia

49
00:01:53,495 --> 00:01:56,855
major with no combat experience: George

50
00:01:56,855 --> 00:01:57,545
Washington.

51
00:01:58,270 --> 00:02:00,410
That winter, Washington traveled hundreds

52
00:02:00,410 --> 00:02:02,930
of miles through snow and forest to Fort

53
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:04,760
Le Boeuf, near Lake Erie,

54
00:02:05,270 --> 00:02:06,400
and handed over the letter.

55
00:02:06,944 --> 00:02:09,584
His journal of the trip was printed in Williamsburg

56
00:02:09,584 --> 00:02:12,154
and in London. Almost overnight,

57
00:02:12,544 --> 00:02:14,604
the young major's name was known on both

58
00:02:14,604 --> 00:02:15,894
sides of the Atlantic.

59
00:02:16,625 --> 00:02:19,325
The French reply was polite and absolutely

60
00:02:19,325 --> 00:02:22,205
clear: "As to the summons you send me to

61
00:02:22,205 --> 00:02:24,845
retire, I do not think myself obliged to

62
00:02:24,845 --> 00:02:25,535
obey it."

63
00:02:26,853 --> 00:02:28,933
Dinwiddie made Washington a lieutenant colonel

64
00:02:28,933 --> 00:02:31,573
and ordered a fort built at the Forks of

65
00:02:31,573 --> 00:02:32,043
the Ohio.

66
00:02:32,958 --> 00:02:36,538
In February 1754, a small Virginian party

67
00:02:36,538 --> 00:02:39,188
began the work. Two months later,

68
00:02:39,398 --> 00:02:41,408
about five hundred French soldiers,

69
00:02:41,778 --> 00:02:44,018
militia and Native allies came down the

70
00:02:44,018 --> 00:02:44,988
Allegheny River.

71
00:02:45,481 --> 00:02:47,991
The thirty-six Virginians had no choice.

72
00:02:48,761 --> 00:02:49,741
They handed over the site,

73
00:02:49,741 --> 00:02:51,601
and the French began building their own

74
00:02:51,601 --> 00:02:53,892
stronghold: Fort Duquesne.

75
00:02:54,192 --> 00:02:56,532
Their commander, Captain Contrecœur,

76
00:02:56,532 --> 00:02:59,002
was under orders not to attack unless provoked.

77
00:02:59,812 --> 00:03:02,292
Instead, he sent a small party to find the

78
00:03:02,292 --> 00:03:04,612
Virginians and deliver a formal summons

79
00:03:04,612 --> 00:03:05,362
to leave.

80
00:03:06,044 --> 00:03:08,064
By then, Washington was already marching

81
00:03:08,064 --> 00:03:10,954
west with fewer than 160 men,

82
00:03:11,384 --> 00:03:13,664
under orders that one historian later called

83
00:03:13,664 --> 00:03:15,934
"an invitation to start a war."

84
00:03:16,470 --> 00:03:18,130
He camped at a clearing called the Great

85
00:03:18,130 --> 00:03:20,840
Meadows. And there, he received a message.

86
00:03:22,374 --> 00:03:24,854
On May 27, the frontiersman Christopher

87
00:03:24,854 --> 00:03:28,474
Gist rode in with news: a French party was

88
00:03:28,474 --> 00:03:29,744
moving through the area.

89
00:03:30,278 --> 00:03:32,118
It came from Tanacharison,

90
00:03:32,118 --> 00:03:33,938
a Mingo leader the British called the Half

91
00:03:33,938 --> 00:03:36,158
King. He had found a French camp,

92
00:03:36,158 --> 00:03:38,528
hidden in a ravine a few miles away.

93
00:03:39,052 --> 00:03:41,732
Its commander was Ensign Joseph Coulon de

94
00:03:41,732 --> 00:03:44,192
Villiers de Jumonville, with about thirty-five

95
00:03:44,192 --> 00:03:47,312
men. He carried a written summons ordering

96
00:03:47,312 --> 00:03:48,662
the Virginians to leave,

97
00:03:49,192 --> 00:03:51,172
the same kind of message Washington had

98
00:03:51,172 --> 00:03:52,382
delivered months earlier.

99
00:03:53,069 --> 00:03:54,579
Through a night of pouring rain,

100
00:03:54,889 --> 00:03:57,789
Washington and forty men followed Tanacharison's

101
00:03:57,789 --> 00:03:59,659
warriors through the dark forest.

102
00:04:00,369 --> 00:04:02,449
At dawn on May 28,

103
00:04:02,449 --> 00:04:04,429
they took positions behind the rocks above

104
00:04:04,429 --> 00:04:05,379
the French camp.

105
00:04:05,949 --> 00:04:08,159
Someone saw them. A shot rang out.

106
00:04:08,969 --> 00:04:10,419
Washington gave the order to fire.

107
00:04:11,007 --> 00:04:12,917
The Virginians fired from the rocks.

108
00:04:13,707 --> 00:04:15,457
Some of the French grabbed their muskets.

109
00:04:15,947 --> 00:04:17,107
Others ran for the woods,

110
00:04:17,107 --> 00:04:19,067
straight into Tanacharison's warriors,

111
00:04:19,067 --> 00:04:20,637
and turned back to surrender.

112
00:04:21,368 --> 00:04:22,428
In about fifteen minutes,

113
00:04:22,428 --> 00:04:24,828
it was over. Jumonville and nine of his

114
00:04:24,828 --> 00:04:27,438
men were dead. Twenty-one were taken prisoner.

115
00:04:28,488 --> 00:04:29,658
Washington lost one man.

116
00:04:30,196 --> 00:04:32,656
Afterward, he wrote to his brother: "I heard

117
00:04:32,656 --> 00:04:34,456
the bullets whistle, and believe me,

118
00:04:34,456 --> 00:04:36,606
there was something charming in the sound."

119
00:04:38,034 --> 00:04:40,214
But how Jumonville died became one of the

120
00:04:40,214 --> 00:04:42,574
most disputed moments in early American

121
00:04:42,574 --> 00:04:43,104
history.

122
00:04:43,750 --> 00:04:45,910
French reports said he was shot in the head

123
00:04:45,910 --> 00:04:48,400
while his summons was being read aloud.

124
00:04:49,006 --> 00:04:50,986
Other accounts said Tanacharison himself

125
00:04:50,986 --> 00:04:53,686
stepped up to the wounded officer and killed

126
00:04:53,686 --> 00:04:54,856
him with a hatchet.

127
00:04:55,302 --> 00:04:57,392
Washington's own accounts are brief.

128
00:04:58,162 --> 00:04:59,862
One historian believes they were written

129
00:04:59,862 --> 00:05:01,702
to hide what happened after the shooting

130
00:05:01,702 --> 00:05:02,432
stopped.

131
00:05:03,010 --> 00:05:05,000
To the French, Jumonville was a diplomat,

132
00:05:05,630 --> 00:05:06,940
murdered on a peaceful mission.

133
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:09,050
To the Virginians, he was a spy,

134
00:05:09,050 --> 00:05:11,440
whose men had been shadowing their camp.

135
00:05:12,086 --> 00:05:13,676
The truth may never be known.

136
00:05:14,066 --> 00:05:15,896
But the consequences arrived quickly.

137
00:05:17,507 --> 00:05:19,477
Washington knew the French would come.

138
00:05:20,047 --> 00:05:20,837
At the Great Meadows,

139
00:05:21,067 --> 00:05:22,347
his men threw up a small,

140
00:05:22,347 --> 00:05:25,617
round stockade. He called it Fort Necessity.

141
00:05:26,078 --> 00:05:27,938
It was a poor position: a low,

142
00:05:27,938 --> 00:05:30,338
wet meadow, with wooded high ground inside

143
00:05:30,338 --> 00:05:33,458
musket range. A company of British regulars

144
00:05:33,458 --> 00:05:35,768
from South Carolina joined him,

145
00:05:35,998 --> 00:05:38,348
bringing his force to about four hundred.

146
00:05:38,648 --> 00:05:41,248
On July 3, about six hundred French and

147
00:05:41,248 --> 00:05:44,068
Canadian soldiers and a hundred Native warriors

148
00:05:44,068 --> 00:05:47,148
arrived. Their commander was Louis Coulon

149
00:05:47,148 --> 00:05:49,618
de Villiers: Jumonville's older brother.

150
00:05:50,178 --> 00:05:52,298
Heavy rain flooded the trenches and soaked

151
00:05:52,298 --> 00:05:54,588
the gunpowder. By nightfall,

152
00:05:54,798 --> 00:05:57,038
about one in four of Washington's men had

153
00:05:57,038 --> 00:05:58,368
been killed or wounded.

154
00:05:59,025 --> 00:06:01,465
Washington surrendered. It was the only

155
00:06:01,465 --> 00:06:04,075
time in his military career he ever would.

156
00:06:04,611 --> 00:06:06,121
The terms were written in French,

157
00:06:06,391 --> 00:06:07,921
a language he could not read.

158
00:06:08,791 --> 00:06:11,351
His Dutch translator, Jacob Van Braam,

159
00:06:11,351 --> 00:06:13,021
read them out in the pouring rain.

160
00:06:13,562 --> 00:06:16,042
Buried in the text was one word: the French

161
00:06:16,042 --> 00:06:18,962
for "assassination." Washington had signed

162
00:06:18,962 --> 00:06:21,302
a document admitting that Jumonville had

163
00:06:21,302 --> 00:06:22,052
been assassinated.

164
00:06:22,933 --> 00:06:24,823
The next morning, his men marched out,

165
00:06:24,953 --> 00:06:27,473
carrying their wounded. The date was July

166
00:06:27,473 --> 00:06:31,673
4, 1754: exactly twenty-two years before

167
00:06:31,673 --> 00:06:33,263
the Declaration of Independence.

168
00:06:34,138 --> 00:06:36,658
The French printed it across Europe as proof

169
00:06:36,658 --> 00:06:38,008
of British treachery.

170
00:06:39,273 --> 00:06:41,453
A year later, Britain sent two regiments

171
00:06:41,453 --> 00:06:43,693
under General Edward Braddock to take Fort

172
00:06:43,693 --> 00:06:46,643
Duquesne. Washington rode with him.

173
00:06:47,113 --> 00:06:48,993
It ended in one of the worst British defeats

174
00:06:48,993 --> 00:06:50,083
of the century.

175
00:06:50,645 --> 00:06:53,785
In 1756, the fighting spread to Europe and

176
00:06:53,785 --> 00:06:56,565
became the Seven Years' War: Britain and

177
00:06:56,565 --> 00:06:59,035
Prussia against France, Austria and Russia,

178
00:06:59,445 --> 00:07:00,715
with battles in Europe,

179
00:07:00,825 --> 00:07:02,295
North America, the Caribbean,

180
00:07:02,645 --> 00:07:04,675
India, West Africa and the Philippines.

181
00:07:05,475 --> 00:07:07,655
As the British writer Horace Walpole later

182
00:07:07,655 --> 00:07:11,015
put it: "The volley fired by a young Virginian

183
00:07:11,015 --> 00:07:13,395
in the backwoods of America set the world

184
00:07:13,395 --> 00:07:14,185
on fire."

185
00:07:14,726 --> 00:07:16,796
When it ended in 1763,

186
00:07:17,126 --> 00:07:19,886
France had lost Canada and almost all of

187
00:07:19,886 --> 00:07:21,636
its empire in North America.

188
00:07:22,128 --> 00:07:24,578
For the Native nations of the Ohio Country,

189
00:07:24,908 --> 00:07:26,868
the British victory brought settlers and

190
00:07:26,868 --> 00:07:29,178
broken promises. Within months,

191
00:07:29,468 --> 00:07:31,628
many of them rose against the British in

192
00:07:31,628 --> 00:07:32,738
Pontiac's War.

193
00:07:33,305 --> 00:07:35,415
But victory left Britain deep in debt.

194
00:07:35,945 --> 00:07:36,775
To pay for it,

195
00:07:36,825 --> 00:07:39,175
Parliament began taxing its American colonies:

196
00:07:39,825 --> 00:07:41,795
the Sugar Act, the Stamp Act.

197
00:07:42,393 --> 00:07:44,943
The colonists' anger grew into a revolution.

198
00:07:45,713 --> 00:07:49,333
And in 1775, the man chosen to command the

199
00:07:49,333 --> 00:07:51,473
Continental Army against the British Empire

200
00:07:51,473 --> 00:07:54,073
was the same Virginian who had fired the

201
00:07:54,073 --> 00:07:56,803
first shots for it: George Washington.

202
00:07:58,391 --> 00:08:00,801
A fifteen-minute ambush in a rainy glen.

203
00:08:01,271 --> 00:08:02,501
A dead French officer.

204
00:08:03,111 --> 00:08:05,421
A signature on a document he couldn't read.

205
00:08:06,007 --> 00:08:08,297
From that small moment came a world war,

206
00:08:08,687 --> 00:08:09,777
the end of French America,

207
00:08:10,347 --> 00:08:12,097
and the road to American independence.

208
00:08:13,404 --> 00:08:15,424
Next time: what happened when Washington

209
00:08:15,424 --> 00:08:17,804
marched back into the Ohio Country with

210
00:08:17,804 --> 00:08:19,904
General Braddock, and walked out of the

211
00:08:19,904 --> 00:08:22,514
disaster with four bullet holes in his coat.

